==English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|LL.|en}} {{m|la|recapitulatus}}, past participle of {{m|la|recapitulare||to go over the main points of a thing again}}, from {{etyl|la|en}} {{m|la|re-||again}} + {{m|la|capitulum||a head, main part, chapter}}; see {{m|en|capitulate}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|Canada|UK}} {{IPA|/ɹiːkəˈpɪtʃʊleɪt/|lang=en}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|recapitulat}}

# To [[summarize]] or repeat in [[concise]] form.
#: ''The entire symphony was '''recapitulated''' in the last four bars.''
# {{lb|en|biology|of an organism}} During an [[individual|individual's]] [[development]], to pass through stages corresponding to the [[species|species']] stages of [[evolutionary]] development.
#* '''1997''', G. A. Bray, "Growth of a Molecular Base for Feeding," ''Obesity Research'', vol. 5, no. 3 (May), p. 272:
#*: Similarly this concept of unity provided a powerful impetus for embryological studies and the idea that fetal development '''recapitulates''' the steps of phylogenetic development.

